      "headline": "Twenty-cent USB graphics card drives an old analog TV",
      "body": "Bitluni built a USB to analog video converter using a 13-cent RISC-V microcontroller and a few passive components. The PCB itself forms both the USB-C and RCA connectors, and the device outputs grayscale text and simple graphics. A crystal oscillator keeps the signal timed to the display's refresh rate.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that useful video output can be squeezed from extremely cheap, minimal hardware, pointing toward ultra-low-cost display solutions.",
